WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.353 --> 00:00:02.231
This is the Automations page.

2
00:00:02.231 --> 00:00:05.968
An automation is a simple rule: when something happens,

3
00:00:05.968 --> 00:00:09.705
the trigger, Cyrus does something for you, the action.

4
00:00:09.705 --> 00:00:12.550
You set it up once, and Cyrus keeps watching.

5
00:00:13.217 --> 00:00:16.106
The badge on the right is the automation engine.

6
00:00:16.106 --> 00:00:21.106
It checks all your rules about once a minute, and shows when it last finished a check.

7
00:00:21.763 --> 00:00:24.279
If checks run late, it turns amber.

8
00:00:24.279 --> 00:00:29.838
If the engine stops, it turns red, and nothing triggers until you restart Cyrus.

9
00:00:29.838 --> 00:00:32.285
It also flags a check that hit an error.

10
00:00:32.952 --> 00:00:35.429
Rules only run while Cyrus is running.

11
00:00:35.429 --> 00:00:38.774
That's why closing the window keeps Cyrus in the system

12
00:00:38.774 --> 00:00:41.998
tray, or the menu bar on a Mac, instead of quitting.

13
00:00:42.679 --> 00:00:45.262
Each rule belongs to one exchange account.

14
00:00:45.262 --> 00:00:50.634
The Exchange menu chooses whose rules you see, and which account a new rule uses.

15
00:00:50.634 --> 00:00:53.453
Here, it's the Kraken account called Main.

16
00:00:54.139 --> 00:00:56.882
This table shows what each exchange supports.

17
00:00:56.882 --> 00:01:00.086
Every exchange can convert one coin into another.

18
00:01:00.086 --> 00:01:05.698
Withdrawing to a saved address needs API support, and today that's Kraken only.

19
00:01:06.369 --> 00:01:09.776
Once you know it, click the header to fold the table away.

20
00:01:10.499 --> 00:01:16.708
The Flow tab draws your automations as a map, read from left to right: the coin a rule sends, a box

21
00:01:16.708 --> 00:01:22.667
for the rule itself, and where the money ends up, either another coin or a withdrawal address.

22
00:01:22.617 --> 00:01:26.473
Each colour is a kind of trigger, and paused rules are dashed.

23
00:01:27.135 --> 00:01:31.017
Click a rule to light up its path, and underneath, Cyrus spells

24
00:01:31.017 --> 00:01:34.529
it out in plain English, with how many times it has run.

25
00:01:34.479 --> 00:01:37.484
From there you can edit it, or pause and resume it.

26
00:01:38.107 --> 00:01:42.863
The Rules tab lists the same automations as a table: name, trigger,

27
00:01:42.863 --> 00:01:47.618
action, whether it's active or paused, and how often it has fired.

28
00:01:48.292 --> 00:01:51.088
Triggered counts every time a rule fired.

29
00:01:51.088 --> 00:01:56.912
For price rules, Success counts how many of those went through, against the limit you set.

30
00:01:57.584 --> 00:02:01.608
On the right, the pencil edits a rule, pause stops it being checked

31
00:02:01.608 --> 00:02:05.752
while keeping its settings, play resumes it, and the bin deletes it.

32
00:02:06.423 --> 00:02:09.205
Allocation rules come from the Balancer page.

33
00:02:09.205 --> 00:02:12.097
They're listed here so you see everything in one

34
00:02:12.097 --> 00:02:15.110
place, and editing one takes you to the Balancer.

35
00:02:15.785 --> 00:02:17.012
Now let's build one.

36
00:02:17.012 --> 00:02:18.474
Click New Automation.

37
00:02:18.474 --> 00:02:23.009
The builder has three steps: when, then, and review.

38
00:02:23.686 --> 00:02:25.245
First, the trigger.

39
00:02:25.245 --> 00:02:28.993
There are three: an order is filled, a balance reaches

40
00:02:28.993 --> 00:02:31.907
a threshold, or a price reaches a target.

41
00:02:32.575 --> 00:02:35.397
An order trigger watches one of your open orders.

42
00:02:35.397 --> 00:02:38.514
When it leaves your open orders, Cyrus looks it up,

43
00:02:38.514 --> 00:02:41.754
and the rule only runs if the order actually filled.

44
00:02:41.754 --> 00:02:43.988
Cancelling it won't set the rule off.

45
00:02:44.667 --> 00:02:49.777
A balance trigger fires when your balance of a coin is at or above the amount you set.

46
00:02:49.777 --> 00:02:54.231
It's handy for sweeping up a coin that builds up, like staking rewards.

47
00:02:54.903 --> 00:02:58.974
Balance and price rules also have a cooldown, the shortest gap

48
00:02:58.974 --> 00:03:03.044
between two runs: at least a minute, and a day to start with.

49
00:03:03.044 --> 00:03:07.326
Without it, a balance that stays high would fire on every check.

50
00:03:07.994 --> 00:03:10.258
The third trigger is a price target.

51
00:03:10.258 --> 00:03:15.417
Pick a coin, and a price in US dollars, USDT or USDC.

52
00:03:15.417 --> 00:03:20.816
The rule fires when the price is at or above your target, so it's made for taking profit.

53
00:03:21.482 --> 00:03:24.623
Prices can spike and fall back between checks.

54
00:03:24.623 --> 00:03:29.345
So rather than only the price at that moment, Cyrus reads the short price

55
00:03:29.345 --> 00:03:33.613
candles since its last check, and uses the highest price reached.

56
00:03:34.282 --> 00:03:37.365
Note that the sale is a market order at the price

57
00:03:37.365 --> 00:03:40.322
when Cyrus acts, which can be below that peak.

58
00:03:40.322 --> 00:03:44.844
And where an exchange has no candle data, it uses the current price.

59
00:03:45.518 --> 00:03:47.130
Click Next for the action.

60
00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:50.335
A price rule always converts the coin it watches.

61
00:03:50.335 --> 00:03:54.590
Choose what into, and how much each time: all of it, a percentage

62
00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:57.928
of your balance at that moment, or a fixed amount.

63
00:03:58.597 --> 00:04:02.988
The execution limit caps how many times the rule may succeed.

64
00:04:02.988 --> 00:04:05.688
Untick Unlimited and enter a number.

65
00:04:05.688 --> 00:04:10.103
When it's reached, Cyrus pauses the rule and notes it in the log.

66
00:04:10.103 --> 00:04:11.832
Failed attempts don't count.

67
00:04:12.494 --> 00:04:16.181
Step three: name the rule, then read the summary.

68
00:04:16.181 --> 00:04:21.713
It spells the whole rule out in one sentence, so you can check it before anything is saved.

69
00:04:22.381 --> 00:04:26.440
Create Automation would save it, and it's checked from the next cycle on.

70
00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:28.621
We'll close this one without saving.

71
00:04:29.297 --> 00:04:33.090
Order and balance triggers can either withdraw or convert.

72
00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:37.119
Let's set up an order rule to see both, on a buy order for ATOM.

73
00:04:37.795 --> 00:04:42.639
Withdraw sends coins to an address you've already whitelisted on the exchange.

74
00:04:42.639 --> 00:04:46.509
The coin is whatever the order brings in: for a buy, the coin

75
00:04:46.509 --> 00:04:50.125
you bought, and for a sell, the currency you're paid in.

76
00:04:50.778 --> 00:04:56.220
Send a fixed amount, or, for a buy order, exactly the amount that filled.

77
00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:59.879
Cyrus also knows each coin's minimum withdrawal, plus

78
00:04:59.879 --> 00:05:03.401
a small safety margin, and won't try to send less.

79
00:05:04.078 --> 00:05:07.299
Convert swaps the coin for another with a market order.

80
00:05:07.299 --> 00:05:10.699
Leave the amount empty, and it converts your whole available

81
00:05:10.699 --> 00:05:14.041
balance of that coin, not just what this order brought in.

82
00:05:14.711 --> 00:05:16.097
We'll close this one too.

83
00:05:16.907 --> 00:05:19.277
To change a rule, click its pencil.

84
00:05:19.277 --> 00:05:24.039
You can always rename it and change its execution limit, and depending

85
00:05:24.039 --> 00:05:28.732
on the trigger, its price or threshold, cooldown, amount or address.

86
00:05:29.406 --> 00:05:32.441
The trigger type and the coin it watches are fixed.

87
00:05:32.441 --> 00:05:34.768
To change those, make a new rule.

88
00:05:34.768 --> 00:05:37.335
Cancel closes this without changes.

89
00:05:38.015 --> 00:05:41.944
The Log tab is the history of every run: when it happened,

90
00:05:41.944 --> 00:05:45.399
what triggered it, what Cyrus did, and the result.

91
00:05:46.073 --> 00:05:49.308
Success means the trade or withdrawal went through.

92
00:05:49.308 --> 00:05:53.137
Error means it was tried and failed, and the result says why.

93
00:05:53.137 --> 00:05:56.316
Skipped means the rule was checked but didn't act.

94
00:05:56.971 --> 00:06:02.063
A skip always gives the reason: a price that hasn't reached its target, with the

95
00:06:02.063 --> 00:06:07.027
highest price seen, a coin under its cap, or a rule waiting out its cooldown.

96
00:06:07.703 --> 00:06:12.600
A rule that keeps skipping for the same reason writes one line, not one a minute.

97
00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:16.975
A new line appears when the reason changes, or when the rule runs.

98
00:06:17.643 --> 00:06:19.189
One safety detail.

99
00:06:19.189 --> 00:06:23.697
Cyrus records that a rule has fired before it contacts the exchange.

100
00:06:23.697 --> 00:06:27.836
That starts the cooldown, so if something fails partway, a balance

101
00:06:27.836 --> 00:06:31.662
or price rule won't fire again a minute later and double up.

102
00:06:32.337 --> 00:06:38.710
If an exchange is busy or limiting requests, Cyrus simply tries again on the next check.

103
00:06:39.377 --> 00:06:44.274
For email alerts, turn on Email Me on Execution in your Profile.

104
00:06:44.274 --> 00:06:47.963
Each time a rule goes through, you'll get the rule, the

105
00:06:47.963 --> 00:06:51.787
time, the trigger, the action and the exchange's result.

106
00:06:52.476 --> 00:06:57.998
If an email can't be sent, the trade still goes ahead, and the log still records it.

107
00:06:58.685 --> 00:07:00.084
That's Automations.

108
00:07:00.084 --> 00:07:06.095
A good first step is one price rule with an execution limit of one, then watch the log.

109
00:07:06.095 --> 00:07:07.850
Next, the Balancer.

